# emem.eio — the whole site. src/eio.mjs compiles it, checks every rule, then runs it.
#
#   verb args              one statement per line
#   verb args <<  ...  >>  a block
#   {x}                    filled from the run
#   kind name : ext ext    what can be read, grouped
#   step name : A -> B     a typed step; the runtime must implement it
#   flow name : a b c      steps in order; each output type must be the next input type
#   rule ...               checked at load; a broken rule stops the page, like a compiler

say     Turn any file into a link your AI agent can read.
in      Drop files, paste text, or paste a URL or GitHub repo
note    Public: anyone with the link can read it.
empty   your link appears here
blank   Nothing to read yet. Drop a file, or paste text, a URL or a GitHub repo.

kind docs   : pdf docx pptx xlsx epub html htm md mdx rst txt
kind data   : csv tsv json jsonl yaml yml toml xml ini sql log
kind code   : js mjs cjs ts tsx jsx py rb go rs java kt swift c h cc cpp hpp cs php sh bash css scss vue svelte lua r jl dart ex exs hs scala eio
kind images : png jpg jpeg webp gif bmp

limit   4000000
section 24000
max     160

step read   : input    -> items
step text   : items    -> docs
step split  : docs     -> sections
step sign   : sections -> signed
step store  : signed   -> stored
step link   : stored   -> link
flow make   : read text split sign store link

step fetch  : url      -> notes
step prove  : notes    -> link
flow open   : fetch prove

give index <<
{index}
>>

give AGENTS.md <<
## Context: {title}
Before writing code, read {link}
{shape} Fetch only the sections the task needs. If it does not say something, do not guess.
>>

give chat <<
Read {link} before you answer. {shape}
When you rely on it, quote it word for word in "double quotes". If it does not say something, say so.
>>

give curl <<
curl -s {link}
curl -s {first}

# each file is named by the hash of its bytes; recompute it (pip install blake3):
curl -s {link} | python3 -c "import sys,blake3,base64;print(base64.b32encode(blake3.blake3(sys.stdin.buffer.read()).digest(16)).decode().rstrip('=').lower())"
# expect {cid}
>>

give MCP <<
claude mcp add --transport http emem https://emem.dev/mcp/full
# then, in any MCP agent: read {link}
emem_memory_view {"file_cid":"{cid}"}
>>

# shown in the index tab before anything is dropped: a real excerpt, so the output is visible first
sample <<
# RFC 9110: HTTP Semantics

> 194-page PDF. ~113k tokens in 23 sections. Read this index, then fetch only the sections your task needs.

## Sections

- [9. Methods · pp. 71–79](…): ~6k tokens · 9.1. Overview; 9.2.1. Safe Methods; 9.2.2. Idempotent Methods; 9.3.1. GET; 9.3.3. POST
- [15. Status Codes · pp. 125–133](…): ~6k tokens · 15.1. Overview of Status Codes; 15.3.1. 200 OK; 15.3.2. 201 Created; 15.4. Redirection 3xx
- [15.4.1. 300 Multiple Choices · pp. 133–142](…): ~6k tokens · 15.5.1. 400 Bad Request; 15.5.4. 403 Forbidden; 15.5.21. 422 Unprocessable Content
  … one line per section. Your agent reads this first, then opens only what it needs.
>>

check   Paste your AI's answer. Every "quote" in it is checked against the source.

rule words say 6..12
rule plain say in note empty blank : cid blake3 ed25519 hash signed token emem: sha256 attester bundle
rule typed make open
rule carry give : {link} {index}
